<!--
  Template for a markdown-defined CAP agent, consumed by @cap-js/agents.

  Copy this file to srv/<your-agent-folder>/AGENTS.md next to (or under) the CDS
  service definition it applies to, then fill in the placeholders and delete these
  comments.

  WHAT THIS FILE CONTROLS
  ------------------------
  - The YAML frontmatter below populates the agent's A2A "agent card" — the metadata
    a client (this plugin's chat UI, or any other A2A caller) sees when it discovers
    the agent, e.g. at /a2a/<service>/.well-known/agent-card.json.
  - Everything in the body, below the frontmatter, becomes the agent's SYSTEM PROMPT.
    It is sent to the model on every turn and shapes how the agent behaves, what it
    refuses to do, and how it reports outcomes — write it the way you'd brief a new
    hire on this one service, not as documentation for other developers.

  WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CONTROL
  --------------------------------
  Defining this file changes what the agent's card ADVERTISES, not what it can DO.
  The set of callable tools is still derived directly from the CDS service model
  (every action, every queryable entity) regardless of anything written here or in
  any SKILL.md. An action left out of every skill's description is still callable —
  it just won't be discoverable by scanning the card. Don't rely on omission from
  this file as an access-control mechanism; it isn't one.
-->
---
name: <agent-name>                 # short, stable identifier — used in URLs/logs
version: "1.0.0"                   # bump when behaviour or skills change meaningfully
description: >
  <One to three sentences describing what this agent does and for whom, in the
  voice of a catalog entry — this is what a human or another agent reads to decide
  whether to use it.>
---

# <Agent Display Name>

## Identity

<Who is this agent? What is it responsible for? What tone/style should it use?
E.g. "You are the X Agent for <domain>. You are concise, factual, and never invent
data you have not retrieved via a tool.">

## Behaviour

<Concrete rules the agent must follow. Prefer a short bulleted list over prose:
- What to do when a request is ambiguous.
- What to verify before taking an action (especially any @agent.hitl action).
- How to report success/failure — e.g. "never claim an action succeeded unless the
  tool call returned successfully."
- Anything the agent must refuse to do.

Reference specific skills by name where relevant (see SKILL.md.template) rather
than re-describing their workflow here — keep the workflow detail in the skill.>
